 private static readonly Entry[] Entries=[
        new(103,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U07:L01","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-7/lesson-1/preparation.html","2da4188392d740b0c0d36219b5b548bd82ee75d3ec57a87ee2ed6f1f6ed1f04d",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.A.2","CCSS:2.NBT.B.7","CCSS:2.NBT.B.8"},
            new string[]{"Learning goal: You will add or subtract within 1,000 using number relationships.\nIn a previous unit, you compared three-digit numbers using a number line and considered how close you were to one another and your distance from zero. When locating numbers on the number line, you recognize that when the numbers are relatively close, you can simply count on or count back to determine the difference between the two numbers. In the second activity, you analyze number lines and counting sequences that increase or decrease by 10 or 100.",
            "Key ideas:\n- Add and subtract within 1,000 using number relationships\n- In a previous unit, you compared three-digit numbers using a number line and considered how close you were to one another and your distance from zero\n- Measurement units must be equal in size and placed or interpreted without gaps, overlaps, or a shifted starting point.",
            "Example 1: Find the value of each expression mentally. \\(586 - 6\\) \\(586 - 8\\) \\(434 - 5\\) \\(352 - 4\\)\nApproach: Represent the whole and the part, write a matching subtraction relationship, then find and check the difference.\nExample 2: Tyler and Elena were asked to find the value of \\(81 - 79\\) . Their work is shown. Tyler \\(\\begin{align} 81-70 &=11 \\\\ 11-9&= 2 \\\\ \\end{align} \\) Elena \\(81 - 79 = 2\\) What do you notice? What do you wonder? Locate and label 203 and 198 on the number line. Compare using >, <, or =. \\(\\underline{\\hspace{1.5cm}} \\quad\\boxed{\\phantom{\\huge00}} \\quad...\nApproach: Represent the whole and the part, write a matching subtraction relationship, then find and check the difference.",
            "Step 1: Identify the whole and the part being removed or compared.\nStep 2: Represent the situation with objects, a drawing, number line, or equation.\nStep 3: Write a subtraction relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the difference using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check the result with the original quantities or a related addition fact.",
            "Common mistake: mixing up the whole and the part being removed or compared. Check that the subtraction relationship matches the representation.",
            "You will add or subtract within 1,000 using number relationships. In a previous unit, you compared three-digit numbers using a number line and considered how close you were to one another and your distance from zero."},
            new string[]{"IM2021 Grade2 Unit7 Lesson1: Compare, Count on, and Count Back. Compare nearby three-digit values on accurately scaled number lines; a short distance can be found by counting on or back. Equal tick gaps represent equal tens or hundreds. ACTUAL PUBLIC ORIGINAL TASKS: Warm-up 586−6, 586−8, 434−5, 352−4. Activity 1 contrasts Tyler base-ten subtraction and Elena counting on for 81−79; locate 203/198, 673/680, 501/499 and solve 400−396. Activity 2 extends number lines 234,334,434,?,?; 502,?,702,?,902; ?,?,332,342,352; 278,?,478,578,?; and 739,749,?,769,?. Human review boundary: Real student-labelled number lines, paired comparison methods and explanations need educator review; the publisher Cool-down requires sign-in.",
            "Place-value lesson rules: Compare nearby three-digit values on accurately scaled number lines; a short distance can be found by counting on or back. Equal tick gaps represent equal tens or hundreds. Official source-aligned outcome references (not academic acceptance): CCSS:2.NBT.A.2, CCSS:2.NBT.B.7, CCSS:2.NBT.B.8. First-party practice is independent and limited to mathematical numeric checks.",
            "Public publisher task mathematics (not replicated artwork): Warm-up 586−6, 586−8, 434−5, 352−4. Activity 1 contrasts Tyler base-ten subtraction and Elena counting on for 81−79; locate 203/198, 673/680, 501/499 and solve 400−396. Activity 2 extends number lines 234,334,434,?,?; 502,?,702,?,902; ?,?,332,342,352; 278,?,478,578,?; and 739,749,?,769,?. Worked example: 203−198=5 by 198→200 (+2), 200→203 (+3), total 5; 502→602→702 shows successive +100 jumps. These two demonstrations illustrate methods, not source Cool-down. Source media or open authored work must be reviewed by the educator.",
            "1. Identify both numbers and order them. 2. Mark numbers accurately on a number line with a consistent interval. 3. If values are close, count up from smaller to larger or back from larger to smaller. 4. For 10- and 100-jumps keep the other place-value digits unchanged unless crossing a boundary. 5. Explain how both strategies reach the same difference and check with addition.",
            "Common misconception: Do not mistake the size of the numbers for the distance between them, or use unequal tick intervals. For 501−499 the answer is 2, not 102. A +100 jump from 434 gives 534; a +10 jump gives 444. Evidence boundary: Real student-labelled number lines, paired comparison methods and explanations need educator review; the publisher Cool-down requires sign-in.",
            "Grade2 Unit7 Lesson1: Compare nearby three-digit values on accurately scaled number lines; a short distance can be found by counting on or back. Equal tick gaps represent equal tens or hundreds. Real student-labelled number lines, paired comparison methods and explanations need educator review; the publisher Cool-down requires sign-in."}),
        new(104,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U07:L02","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-7/lesson-2/preparation.html","b82d311093f66de2c059a2a34451df799df4af84983e4316795dea3702fe30d0",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.B.5","CCSS:2.NBT.B.7"},
            new string[]{"Learning goal: You will add and subtract multiples of 10 and 100 within 1,000.\nIn grade 1, you added and subtracted multiples of 10 within 100. In a previous unit, you represented three-digit numbers with base-ten blocks, drawings, and words. You used equations to represent three-digit numbers as sums of the value of hundreds, tens, and ones using the number and name of each unit (235 = 2 hundreds + 3 tens + 5 ones) and using expanded form ( \\(235 = 200 + 30 + 5\\) ).",
            "Key ideas:\n- Add and subtract multiples of 10 or 100 to/from a three-digit number\n- In grade 1, you added and subtracted multiples of 10 within 100\n- Measurement units must be equal in size and placed or interpreted without gaps, overlaps, or a shifted starting point.",
            "Example 1: Find the value of each expression mentally. \\(34 + 20\\) \\(34 + 60\\) \\(58 + 30\\) \\(158 + 40\\)\nApproach: Represent the whole and the part, write a matching subtraction relationship, then find and check the difference.\nExample 2: Use your base-ten blocks to show each number. Then, roll a number cube to see how many tens or hundreds to add or subtract. Show 297. Add ___ hundreds. Complete the equation: \\(297 + \\underline{\\hspace{1cm}} =\\underline{\\hspace{1cm}}\\) Show 432. Add ___ tens. \\(432 + \\underline{\\hspace{1cm}} =\\underline{\\hspace{1cm}}\\) Show 982. Subtract ___ tens. \\(982...\nApproach: \\(805 - 400 = 405\\) “How does the equation connect to the diagram or blocks?” (You take away 4 hundreds from the blocks and you’re left with 4 hundreds and 5 ones.) “What did you notice about how the value of the blocks changed?” (There are fewer blocks. The hundreds place changed because you subtracted hundreds.)",
            "Step 1: Identify the whole and the part being removed or compared.\nStep 2: Represent the situation with objects, a drawing, number line, or equation.\nStep 3: Write a subtraction relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the difference using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check the result with the original quantities or a related addition fact.",
            "Common mistake: mixing up the whole and the part being removed or compared. Check that the subtraction relationship matches the representation.",
            "You will add and subtract multiples of 10 and 100 within 1,000. In grade 1, you added and subtracted multiples of 10 within 100."},
            new string[]{"IM2021 Grade2 Unit7 Lesson2: Add and Subtract with Tens and Hundreds. Add or subtract multiples of ten or hundred from three-digit numbers using base-ten units; this lesson deliberately avoids composition or decomposition in the source activity. ACTUAL PUBLIC ORIGINAL TASKS: Warm-up 34+20,34+60,58+30,158+40. Activity 1 students roll number cubes: start 297 and add hundreds, 432 and add tens, 982 and subtract tens, 351 and add hundreds, 805 and subtract hundreds. Activity 2 with actual block stories: 223+400=623,428−200=228,836+60=896,572−20=552,658+300=958,482−300=182. Human review boundary: Source random dice rolls, physical base-ten blocks, original drawings and partner representations need human inspection; no predetermined single source cube outcome.",
            "Place-value lesson rules: Add or subtract multiples of ten or hundred from three-digit numbers using base-ten units; this lesson deliberately avoids composition or decomposition in the source activity. Official source-aligned outcome references (not academic acceptance): CCSS:2.NBT.B.5, CCSS:2.NBT.B.7. First-party practice is independent and limited to mathematical numeric checks.",
            "Public publisher task mathematics (not replicated artwork): Warm-up 34+20,34+60,58+30,158+40. Activity 1 students roll number cubes: start 297 and add hundreds, 432 and add tens, 982 and subtract tens, 351 and add hundreds, 805 and subtract hundreds. Activity 2 with actual block stories: 223+400=623,428−200=228,836+60=896,572−20=552,658+300=958,482−300=182. Worked example: 482−300=182 by removing three hundreds; 658+300=958 by adding three hundreds, preserving tens and ones. These two demonstrations illustrate methods, not source Cool-down. Source media or open authored work must be reviewed by the educator.",
            "1. Represent a number as hundreds, tens and ones. 2. Identify the count of tens or hundreds added or removed. 3. Change the corresponding place only where no new unit is composed/decomposed. 4. Draw blocks or a place-value diagram and write the equivalent equation. 5. Check the new number is between 0 and 1,000.",
            "Common misconception: Ten is not a hundred: 432+30=462, not 732. Do not manufacture a fixed number-cube roll; many values are valid. Check that ones stay fixed when tens or hundreds are changed. Evidence boundary: Source random dice rolls, physical base-ten blocks, original drawings and partner representations need human inspection; no predetermined single source cube outcome.",
            "Grade2 Unit7 Lesson2: Add or subtract multiples of ten or hundred from three-digit numbers using base-ten units; this lesson deliberately avoids composition or decomposition in the source activity. Source random dice rolls, physical base-ten blocks, original drawings and partner representations need human inspection; no predetermined single source cube outcome."}),
        new(105,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U07:L03","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-7/lesson-3/preparation.html","202d7795eb6b9e9c2737e8375711da40fcb1edf3ee112628a66363693fb71ff3",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.B.7"},
            new string[]{"Learning goal: You will subtract within 1,000 using strategies that demonstrate an understanding of the relationship between addition and subtraction, including counting on and counting back.\nIn previous units, you used counting on or counting back to subtract within 100. In this lesson, you analyze different methods, including counting on and counting back, to subtract within 1,000. You make connections between sums of 10 and sums of 100, for example \\(5 + 5 = 10\\) and \\(50 + 50 = 100\\) .",
            "Key ideas:\n- Subtract within 1,000 using an understanding of the relationship between addition and subtraction\n- In previous units, you used counting on or counting back to subtract within 100\n- Subtraction can represent taking away, comparing quantities, or finding a missing addend; the representation must match the situation.",
            "Example 1: Find the value of each expression mentally. \\(120 + 20\\) \\(120 + 200\\) \\(124 + 30\\) \\(124 + 300\\)\nApproach: Represent the whole and the part, write a matching subtraction relationship, then find and check the difference.\nExample 2: Jada and Andre found the value of \\(375 - 24\\) . Here is their work. Jada's Work \\(375 - 24 = 351\\) Andre's Work \\(375 - 20 = 355\\\\ 355 - 4 = 351\\\\ 375 - 24 = 351\\) What do you notice? What do you wonder? Try Andre’s way to find the value of \\(189 - 73\\) . Show your thinking. Use a number line if it helps. Find the value of \\(647 - 46\\) in your own way....\nApproach: Represent the whole and the part, write a matching subtraction relationship, then find and check the difference.",
            "Step 1: Identify the whole and the part being removed or compared.\nStep 2: Represent the situation with objects, a drawing, number line, or equation.\nStep 3: Write a subtraction relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the difference using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check the result with the original quantities or a related addition fact.",
            "Common mistake: mixing up the whole and the part being removed or compared. Check that the subtraction relationship matches the representation.",
            "You will subtract within 1,000 using strategies that demonstrate an understanding of the relationship between addition and subtraction, including counting on and counting back. In previous units, you used counting on or counting back to subtract within 100."},
            new string[]{"IM2021 Grade2 Unit7 Lesson3: Count on or Count Back to Subtract. Subtract by counting back by place and use related unknown-addend equations to count on to a whole hundred or thousand. ACTUAL PUBLIC ORIGINAL TASKS: Warm-up 120+20,120+200,124+30,124+300. Activity 1 compare Jada base-ten and Andre number-line solutions 375−24=351; try 189−73 and 647−46. Activity 2 paint-splatter unknowns include ?+540=1,000; 250+?=1,000; 600−440=?; 680+?=900; ?+590=1,000; 900−370=?. Human review boundary: Publisher splatter artwork is not copied; student number-line drawings and discussions are human-reviewed; Cool-down login required.",
            "Place-value lesson rules: Subtract by counting back by place and use related unknown-addend equations to count on to a whole hundred or thousand. Official source-aligned outcome references (not academic acceptance): CCSS:2.NBT.B.7. First-party practice is independent and limited to mathematical numeric checks.",
            "Public publisher task mathematics (not replicated artwork): Warm-up 120+20,120+200,124+30,124+300. Activity 1 compare Jada base-ten and Andre number-line solutions 375−24=351; try 189−73 and 647−46. Activity 2 paint-splatter unknowns include ?+540=1,000; 250+?=1,000; 600−440=?; 680+?=900; ?+590=1,000; 900−370=?. Worked example: 375−24=351 by subtracting two tens and four ones. The missing addend in ?+540=1,000 is 460 because 540+460=1,000. These two demonstrations illustrate methods, not source Cool-down. Source media or open authored work must be reviewed by the educator.",
            "1. Rewrite subtrahend as hundreds+tens+ones. 2. Subtract in separate jumps on an open number line or by units, or count up from known addend to total. 3. For a hidden addend solve known+?=whole by the difference. 4. Compare the number-line jumps with a place-value model. 5. Check by addition against the original whole.",
            "Common misconception: A smudged equation is not asking for a visual paint shape: ?+540=1,000 needs 460. Preserve the true position of the hidden part and check against the original equality. Evidence boundary: Publisher splatter artwork is not copied; student number-line drawings and discussions are human-reviewed; Cool-down login required.",
            "Grade2 Unit7 Lesson3: Subtract by counting back by place and use related unknown-addend equations to count on to a whole hundred or thousand. Publisher splatter artwork is not copied; student number-line drawings and discussions are human-reviewed; Cool-down login required."}),
        new(106,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U07:L04","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-7/lesson-4/preparation.html","05139b3a1a6b7f9b49bad0ef50c0565ccbd38b3b9dcccb138deaabe586217dea",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.B.7","CCSS:2.NBT.B.8","CCSS:2.NBT.B.9"},
            new string[]{"Learning goal: You will add and subtract within 1,000 without composing a ten or hundred using place value understanding, properties of operations, and the relationship between addition and subtraction.\nIn previous lessons, you used what you know about counting within 1,000 and place value to subtract multiples of 10 and 100 from three-digit numbers. In this lesson, you continue to apply methods you used when adding and subtracting within 100 to add and subtract within 1,000. You also make sense of methods that are represented with equations.",
            "Key ideas:\n- Add and subtract numbers within 1,000 using strategies that do not include composing or decomposing tens or hundreds\n- In previous lessons, you used what you know about counting within 1,000 and place value to subtract multiples of 10 and 100 from three-digit numbers\n- Subtraction can represent taking away, comparing quantities, or finding a missing addend; the representation must match the situation.",
            "Example 1: Find the value of each expression mentally. \\(586 - 100\\) \\(486 - 20\\) \\(457 - 200\\) \\(257 - 30\\)\nApproach: “How can the last 2 expressions help us think about this problem?” (Even though it is 230 being taken away, we can still subtract the hundreds and then the tens.)\nExample 2: Mai and Lin were asked to find the value of \\(500 - 387\\) . Here is their work. Mai's Work Description: Number line. No tick marks. Arrow from point at 387 to point at 487, labeled 100. Arrow from point at 487 to point at 497, labeled 10. Arrow from point at 497 to point at 500, labeled 3. \\(387 + {?} = 500\\) \\(387 + 100 = 487\\) \\(487 + 10 = 497\\) \\(497...\nApproach: “Which method do you prefer? Explain.” (I prefer counting on because you count until you get to the number. When you count on, you can do it in parts so you can make each part something that is easy for you and get to a ten. The number line helped me. I prefer the equations because it helps me see the parts as you are taken away.)",
            "Step 1: Identify the whole and the part being removed or compared.\nStep 2: Represent the situation with objects, a drawing, number line, or equation.\nStep 3: Write a subtraction relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the difference using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check the result with the original quantities or a related addition fact.",
            "Common mistake: mixing up the whole and the part being removed or compared. Check that the subtraction relationship matches the representation.",
            "You will add and subtract within 1,000 without composing a ten or hundred using place value understanding, properties of operations, and the relationship between addition and subtraction. In previous lessons, you used what you know about counting within 1,000 and place value to subtract multiples of 10 and 100 from three-digit numbers."},
            new string[]{"IM2021 Grade2 Unit7 Lesson4: Add and Subtract Three-digit Numbers in Different Ways PLC Activity. Use counting on to a friendly hundred, expanded form, and place-value addition/subtraction; compare mathematically equivalent student strategies. ACTUAL PUBLIC ORIGINAL TASKS: Warm-up 586−100,486−20,457−200,257−30. Activity 1 compare Mai counting up and Lin decomposing 500−387=113; solve 600−476 and 400−134. Activity 2 compare 427+351=778 as expanded-form and place-by-place calculation; solve 725+243,863−432,163+326,692−571 using choice of valid strategies. Human review boundary: Original worked-method images, partner discussions and students' selected method explanations require teacher review; only numerical checks are automatically marked.",
            "Place-value lesson rules: Use counting on to a friendly hundred, expanded form, and place-value addition/subtraction; compare mathematically equivalent student strategies. Official source-aligned outcome references (not academic acceptance): CCSS:2.NBT.B.7, CCSS:2.NBT.B.8, CCSS:2.NBT.B.9. First-party practice is independent and limited to mathematical numeric checks.",
            "Public publisher task mathematics (not replicated artwork): Warm-up 586−100,486−20,457−200,257−30. Activity 1 compare Mai counting up and Lin decomposing 500−387=113; solve 600−476 and 400−134. Activity 2 compare 427+351=778 as expanded-form and place-by-place calculation; solve 725+243,863−432,163+326,692−571 using choice of valid strategies. Worked example: 500−387=113 by counting from 387 to 400 (+13), then 400 to 500 (+100); 427+351=778 by hundreds, tens, ones. These two demonstrations illustrate methods, not source Cool-down. Source media or open authored work must be reviewed by the educator.",
            "1. Decide whether counting on or breaking each number into hundreds, tens, ones is efficient. 2. Align equal place units for an expanded-form calculation. 3. Calculate each part and combine; when using a number line, count jump lengths to the whole. 4. Compare two different valid strategies and explain their equivalence. 5. Verify sum or difference with a reverse relationship.",
            "Common misconception: Do not subtract larger subtrahends by ignoring the required place-unit exchange: 500−387 is 113, not 213. Expanded-form regrouping and counting on must yield the same value. Evidence boundary: Original worked-method images, partner discussions and students' selected method explanations require teacher review; only numerical checks are automatically marked.",
            "Grade2 Unit7 Lesson4: Use counting on to a friendly hundred, expanded form, and place-value addition/subtraction; compare mathematically equivalent student strategies. Original worked-method images, partner discussions and students' selected method explanations require teacher review; only numerical checks are automatically marked."})
